  <!-- COURSE -->

  <div id="courseSection" class="section course-section">

    <h2>📖 Sermon Preparation Bible Study</h2>

    <div class="course-intro">

      <h3>What This Course Will Teach You</h3>

      <p>
        This is a learning course, not just a checklist.
        Each lesson explains what you are doing, how to do it,
        why it matters, questions to ask, and gives you something
        to practice.
      </p>

      <p>
        Work through the lessons in order when you are learning.
        Experienced preachers can use the lessons as a study reference.
      </p>

    </div>


    <!-- LESSON 1 -->

    <div class="lesson">

      <div class="lesson-header">
        <h3>Lesson 1 — Begin With Prayer and the Right Purpose</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="lesson-body">

        <h4>What are you learning?</h4>

        <p>
          A sermon begins with God's Word, not simply with an interesting idea.
          Before studying or writing, take time to pray and ask God for wisdom,
          understanding, humility, and faithfulness to Scripture.
        </p>

        <h4>How do you do it?</h4>

        <p>
          Ask God to help you understand the passage correctly and communicate
          it clearly. Be willing to let Scripture correct your own assumptions.
        </p>

        <h4>Why does this matter?</h4>

        <p>
          The preacher's responsibility is not to make Scripture say whatever
          he wants. The responsibility is to understand and communicate what
          Scripture actually teaches.
        </p>

        <h4>Questions to ask</h4>

        <ul>
          <li>Am I willing to teach what the passage actually says?</li>
          <li>Am I studying Scripture or merely looking for verses supporting my idea?</li>
          <li>What do I want people to understand from God's Word?</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="practice">
          <strong>Practice:</strong>
          Write a short prayer asking God to help you prepare this sermon faithfully.
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- LESSON 2 -->

    <div class="lesson">

      <div class="lesson-header">
        <h3>Lesson 2 — Choose Your Topic or Passage</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="lesson-body">

        <h4>What are you learning?</h4>

        <p>
          A sermon needs a clear subject. You may begin with a biblical passage,
          a biblical doctrine, a Christian life issue, or a need within your
          audience.
        </p>

        <h4>How do you choose?</h4>

        <p>
          Start with Scripture. If you begin with a topic such as faith,
          forgiveness, prayer, salvation, or obedience, find passages that
          actually address that subject.
        </p>

        <div class="example">
          <strong>Example:</strong>
          Instead of starting with "I want to talk about being positive,"
          begin with a biblical question such as:
          "What does Scripture teach about trusting God during difficulty?"
        </div>

        <h4>Questions to ask</h4>

        <ul>
          <li>What is my subject?</li>
          <li>What passage best addresses it?</li>
          <li>Can I explain my topic clearly in one sentence?</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="practice">
          <strong>Practice:</strong>
          Write one possible sermon topic and one Scripture passage you could study.
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- LESSON 3 -->

    <div class="lesson">

      <div class="lesson-header">
        <h3>Lesson 3 — Read the Passage Carefully</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="lesson-body">

        <h4>What are you learning?</h4>

        <p>
          Before looking at commentaries or other resources, spend time reading
          the biblical passage itself.
        </p>

        <h4>How do you study it?</h4>

        <p>
          Read the passage several times. Read before it and after it.
          Look for repeated words, commands, promises, contrasts, people,
          places, causes, results, and important statements.
        </p>

        <h4>Questions to ask</h4>

        <ul>
          <li>Who is speaking?</li>
          <li>Who is being addressed?</li>
          <li>What is happening?</li>
          <li>What words or ideas are repeated?</li>
          <li>What does the passage clearly say?</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="practice">
          <strong>Practice:</strong>
          Read your passage at least three times and write down five observations.
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- LESSON 4 -->

    <div class="lesson">

      <div class="lesson-header">
        <h3>Lesson 4 — Study the Context</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="lesson-body">

        <h4>Why context matters</h4>

        <p>
          A verse should be understood in the context of the surrounding passage,
          book, author, audience, and biblical message.
        </p>

        <h4>How do you study context?</h4>

        <p>
          Read the surrounding chapters. Determine what happens before and after
          your passage. Look at who wrote the book and who originally received it.
        </p>

        <h4>Questions to ask</h4>

        <ul>
          <li>What happens immediately before this passage?</li>
          <li>What happens immediately afterward?</li>
          <li>Who wrote this?</li>
          <li>Who was the original audience?</li>
          <li>What problem, question, or situation is being addressed?</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="practice">
          <strong>Practice:</strong>
          Write three sentences explaining the context of your passage.
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- LESSON 5 -->

    <div class="lesson">

      <div class="lesson-header">
        <h3>Lesson 5 — Find the Main Biblical Truth</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="lesson-body">

        <h4>What is the main truth?</h4>

        <p>
          After observing and understanding the passage, determine the central
          biblical truth you believe the passage is communicating.
        </p>

        <h4>How do you find it?</h4>

        <p>
          Ask what the passage is mainly teaching. Try to summarize that teaching
          in one clear sentence.
        </p>

        <div class="example">
          <strong>Example:</strong>
          Instead of saying "This passage is about Peter,"
          you might discover that the passage teaches:
          "Jesus calls His followers to trust Him even when circumstances create fear."
        </div>

        <h4>Questions to ask</h4>

        <ul>
          <li>What is the author trying to communicate?</li>
          <li>What is the central truth?</li>
          <li>Can I state the truth in one sentence?</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="practice">
          <strong>Practice:</strong>
          Write the main biblical truth of your passage in one sentence.
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- LESSON 6 -->

    <div class="lesson">

      <div class="lesson-header">
        <h3>Lesson 6 — Study Important Hebrew and Greek Words</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="lesson-body">

        <h4>Why study original-language words?</h4>

        <p>
          Hebrew and Greek word studies can help you understand important
          biblical terms more precisely.
        </p>

        <h4>How should you use word studies?</h4>

        <p>
          Do not build an entire doctrine simply from a dictionary definition.
          Study how the word is used in the passage and in Scripture.
        </p>

        <h4>Questions to ask</h4>

        <ul>
          <li>What does the word mean?</li>
          <li>How is the word used in this passage?</li>
          <li>Does the surrounding context affect the meaning?</li>
          <li>How is the word used elsewhere in Scripture?</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="practice">
          <strong>Practice:</strong>
          Choose one important word from your passage and investigate its meaning.
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- LESSON 7 -->

    <div class="lesson">

      <div class="lesson-header">
        <h3>Lesson 7 — Use a Bible Dictionary</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="lesson-body">

        <p>
          A Bible dictionary can help explain people, places, customs,
          biblical subjects, objects, and historical information.
        </p>

        <h4>How does this help?</h4>

        <p>
          Understanding the world behind the passage can make the Scripture
          easier to explain accurately.
        </p>

        <div class="practice">
          <strong>Practice:</strong>
          Look up one person, place, subject, or cultural term connected
          to your passage.
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- LESSON 8 -->

    <div class="lesson">

      <div class="lesson-header">
        <h3>Lesson 8 — Use a Concordance and Cross-References</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="lesson-body">

        <p>
          A concordance helps you find where biblical words occur.
          Cross-references can help you compare related passages.
        </p>

        <h4>How should you use them?</h4>

        <p>
          Find other Scriptures that discuss the same subject, phrase,
          promise, command, or biblical truth.
        </p>

        <div class="practice">
          <strong>Practice:</strong>
          Find at least three related Scriptures and explain how each one
          connects to your sermon passage.
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- LESSON 9 -->

    <div class="lesson">

      <div class="lesson-header">
        <h3>Lesson 9 — Compare Bible Translations</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="lesson-body">

        <p>
          Comparing translations can help you notice wording differences
          and understand how translators communicate the same passage.
        </p>

        <h4>Important principle</h4>

        <p>
          Comparing translations should help you understand the original
          biblical text, not allow you to choose whichever wording supports
          your preferred interpretation.
        </p>

        <div class="practice">
          <strong>Practice:</strong>
          Compare your main passage in at least two translations.
          Write down any important wording differences.
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- LESSON 10 -->

    <div class="lesson">

      <div class="lesson-header">
        <h3>Lesson 10 — Read Bible Commentaries</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="lesson-body">

        <p>
          Commentaries allow you to learn how other Bible teachers and scholars
          have understood the passage.
        </p>

        <h4>How should you use them?</h4>

        <p>
          Study the passage yourself first. Then use commentaries to test,
          expand, and challenge your understanding.
        </p>

        <h4>Questions to ask</h4>

        <ul>
          <li>Does this interpretation fit the context?</li>
          <li>Does it agree with the rest of Scripture?</li>
          <li>What evidence does the commentator provide?</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="practice">
          <strong>Practice:</strong>
          Read at least one commentary on your main passage and record
          something useful you learned.
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- LESSON 11 -->

    <div class="lesson">

      <div class="lesson-header">
        <h3>Lesson 11 — Develop the Main Sermon Point</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="lesson-body">

        <p>
          Your sermon needs one central message. Every major point should
          support that message.
        </p>

        <div class="example">
          <strong>Example:</strong><br>
          Main truth: "God calls us to trust Him when we cannot see the outcome."
          <br><br>
          Point 1: Why we struggle to trust.<br>
          Point 2: What Scripture teaches about trusting God.<br>
          Point 3: How we can practice trust.
        </div>

        <div class="practice">
          <strong>Practice:</strong>
          Write your sermon’s main truth in one sentence.
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- LESSON 12 -->

    <div class="lesson">

      <div class="lesson-header">
        <h3>Lesson 12 — Build Your Supporting Points</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="lesson-body">

        <p>
          Most sermons need several supporting points that help explain the
          main truth.
        </p>

        <h4>Good sermon points should:</h4>

        <ul>
          <li>Come from or clearly support Scripture.</li>
          <li>Move the listener toward the main truth.</li>
          <li>Be easy to understand.</li>
          <li>Have a clear explanation and application.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="practice">
          <strong>Practice:</strong>
          Write three possible sermon points and explain how each supports
          your main biblical truth.
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- LESSON 13 -->

    <div class="lesson">

      <div class="lesson-header">
        <h3>Lesson 13 — Explain the Scripture</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="lesson-body">

        <p>
          Do not simply read a verse and immediately tell people what to do.
          Explain what the verse means in its context.
        </p>

        <h4>A useful pattern:</h4>

        <ol>
          <li>Read the Scripture.</li>
          <li>Explain what it means.</li>
          <li>Show why it matters.</li>
          <li>Apply it to life.</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="practice">
          <strong>Practice:</strong>
          Choose one verse and explain it in your own words without changing
          its meaning.
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- LESSON 14 -->

    <div class="lesson">

      <div class="lesson-header">
        <h3>Lesson 14 — Develop Practical Application</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="lesson-body">

        <p>
          Application answers the question:
          <strong>"What should we do with what Scripture teaches?"</strong>
        </p>

        <h4>Think about:</h4>

        <ul>
          <li>What should the listener believe?</li>
          <li>What should the listener change?</li>
          <li>What should the listener begin doing?</li>
          <li>What should the listener stop doing?</li>
          <li>How does this truth affect everyday life?</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="practice">
          <strong>Practice:</strong>
          Write two practical ways your sermon truth can be lived out.
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- LESSON 15 -->

    <div class="lesson">

      <div class="lesson-header">
        <h3>Lesson 15 — Write the Introduction</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="lesson-body">

        <p>
          The introduction prepares the listener to hear the message.
          It should introduce the subject and create a reason to listen.
        </p>

        <h4>A simple introduction can:</h4>

        <ul>
          <li>Introduce a problem.</li>
          <li>Ask an important question.</li>
          <li>Describe a situation.</li>
          <li>Introduce the passage.</li>
          <li>Explain why the subject matters.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="practice">
          <strong>Practice:</strong>
          Write a short introduction that leads naturally into your main Scripture.
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- LESSON 16 -->

    <div class="lesson">

      <div class="lesson-header">
        <h3>Lesson 16 — Write the Conclusion</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="lesson-body">

        <p>
          The conclusion brings the sermon together and reminds the listener
          of the main biblical truth.
        </p>

        <h4>A conclusion can:</h4>

        <ul>
          <li>Summarize the main points.</li>
          <li>Repeat the central truth.</li>
          <li>Give a final application.</li>
          <li>Call listeners to respond appropriately.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="practice">
          <strong>Practice:</strong>
          Write a short conclusion that brings your sermon back to its main truth.
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- LESSON 17 -->

    <div class="lesson">

      <div class="lesson-header">
        <h3>Lesson 17 — Put the Sermon Together</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="lesson-body">

        <p>
          Now organize everything you have learned into a clear structure.
        </p>

        <h4>A basic sermon structure:</h4>

        <ol>
          <li>Title</li>
          <li>Introduction</li>
          <li>Main Scripture</li>
          <li>Main Biblical Truth</li>
          <li>Point 1</li>
          <li>Point 2</li>
          <li>Point 3</li>
          <li>Applications</li>
          <li>Conclusion</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="practice">
          <strong>Practice:</strong>
          Use the sermon template below to organize your study.
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- LESSON 18 -->

    <div class="lesson">

      <div class="lesson-header">
        <h3>Lesson 18 — Review for Biblical Accuracy and Clarity</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="lesson-body">

        <h4>Before teaching your sermon, review it.</h4>

        <ul>
          <li>Does every major teaching agree with Scripture?</li>
          <li>Did I take verses out of context?</li>
          <li>Is my main point clear?</li>
          <li>Do my points support the main truth?</li>
          <li>Did I explain the Scriptures?</li>
          <li>Are my applications practical?</li>
          <li>Can someone understand the sermon?</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="practice">
          <strong>Final Practice:</strong>
          Read your entire sermon as though you were hearing it for the
          first time. Remove anything unnecessary and clarify anything confusing.
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>

  </div>


  <!-- SERMON TEMPLATE -->

  <div id="templateSection" class="section">

    <h2>📝 Ready-to-Use Sermon Template</h2>

    <p>
      Use this template if you already know how to prepare a sermon,
      or use it while working through the course.
    </p>

    <div class="template-grid">

      <div class="field">
        <label>Sermon Title</label>
        <input id="sermonTitle" placeholder="Enter sermon title">
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <label>Main Scripture</label>
        <input id="mainScripture" placeholder="Example: John 3:16">
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <label>Sermon Topic</label>
        <input id="sermonTopic" placeholder="What is this sermon about?">
      </div>

      <div class="field">
